Home Network Infrastructure Deployment & Security Hardening

This file contains my overall process of installing, configuring, and testing/attacking my household Google Nest Router and mesh topology. It will contain
lessons learned, new discoveries, issues to troubleshoot, topics based, and supportive images to provide context and integrity to my project.

1. Network Deployment
  - Router Installation/Configuration
        The beginning of this project started with intent to upgrade overall LAN/WAN infrastructure by upgrading internet
        speeds from 500 mbps to 1 gbps, as well as establishing a mesh topological network for higher wireless connectivity and less
        deadzones. Thus, the Google Nest Router was purchased along with two other Google Nest Routers to act as wireless
        access points (WAPs). Once routers were delivered, the old router was disconnected, and the new main Google Nest 
        Router was connected to the Quantum Fiber ISP Modem via cat-6 ethernet cable, physically establishing a connection
        capable of over 1 gbps. The Google Home software application was subsequently downloaded and configured with home-owner
        credentials with administrative access to network controls. The new Wi-Fi began running with a new SSID and
        WPA2-PSK security.
  - Mesh Topology Deployment
        The first main office router is placed in the office, on one end of the house, closest to the ISP modem and will provide high connectivity with
        minimum latency for business purposes. Upon successful Wi-Fi development, the two WAPs were plugged in at further points within the household to power up and connect to the main
        router in the office. One WAP was plugged in between the kitchen and living room area with minimal physical interference (i.e. walls, objects, etc.)
        between it and the main office router. The kitchen/livingroom location will benefit connectivity for general activities, as it will provide a primary
        access point for users in popular common spaces. The second and final WAP was placed in the far bedroom in order to extend and optimize effective hot
        zone range. The second WAP will also be feasible for quick outdoor IoT device connectivity. With the three points in place and powered up, they were
        wirelessly connected and tested through the Google Home App, with "Great connection" mesh ratings.
  - Wireless Configuration
        Configuration of the Google Nest Mesh Topology involved
        Google Home App, SSID and PSK, DHCP IP Reservations and DHCP IP Device Pool, Router LAN IP and Subnet Mask; Router and WAP naming, device naming, UPnP,
        setting up device modes for each WAP and main router (NAT or Bridge), Guest Network, tested network speeds, etc.
  - Device Connectivity
        Connecting indivual devices to network via PSK, later using WPA2/WPA3. Tested Device Speeds, Internet connection, communicated with other devices on
        network (personal phone to Smart TV), etc.

