3. Security Hardening
  - Router Configuration Review
        Modem Bridge mode (Double NAT), LAN settings, IP reservations, DHCP Pool for new connections, etc.
  - DNS Security Configuration
        Original DNS security configuration was set to default, where ISP DNS servers were utilized as well as backups. Less reliable.
  - Cloudfare Malware-Blocking DNS
        Default ISP DNS assignment was improved with reassigning DNS settings to Cloudfare DNS servers with malware protection (1.1.1.2, 1.0.0.2).
  - Device Inventory
        Connected devices to network, noting device type and IP, named devices, and assigned static IP reservations for important network devices (security)
        Later used NMAP via Zenmap to scan network devices and obtain in-depth device inventory.
  - Reduction of Unnecessary Exposure
        Mitigate attack surface via segmentation of exposed devices to Guest Network, limiting sharing of resources as well. Increase network encryption
        from WPA2 to WPA2/WPA3. Increase PSK complexity and length. No port forwarding rules, UPnP?, etc.

4. Network Segmentation
  - Trusted/Main Network
        Trusted/Main Network will remain with SSID and PSK for personal sensitive devices as well as critical network infrastructure (i.e. security, printers, etc.)
        Will retain minimum necessary devices with optimized network protocols for safe, efficient network and Internet usage.
  - Guest/IoT Network
        Created Guest Network for guest devices as well as less important or susceptible network devices (i.e. IoT household devices). IoT devices
        such as garage door and pool controller were moved to Guest Network, where they are logically segmented from critical network whilst maintaining
        connectibility to personal phones via app controllers using cloud services. Devices on Guest Network requiring access to Main Network resources
        (i.e. casting to Smart TV), can obtain such access on an as-needed basis via Google Home App.
  - Garage Controller Migration
        Garage Door was deemed exposed via Nmap open port scans, where it was promptly removed from Main Network and added to Guest Network via personal 
        phone application controller. IoT device's Guest Network connectivity was verified via Nmap. Usability through cloud services was tested and verified 
        via personal phone application.
  - Pool Controller Migration
        Pool Controller was deemed susceptible via Nmap open port scans, which was then removed from Main Network and added to Guest Network via personal phone
        application controller. IoT device's Guest Network connectivity was verified via Nmap. Usability through cloud services was tested and verified via 
        personal phone application.
  - Isolation Validation
        Nmap via Zenmap software was used to scan the Guest Network to verify IoT devices connected, as well as new IP address assignments. Segmentation
        prevents Guest devices from accessing Main Network resources, whilst still maintaining Internet access and usability via cloud services.

