6. Nmap - Network & Attack-Surface Assessment
  - Host Discovery
        Nmap host discovery began with first connecting to the network, where I then obtained my IP address through "ipconfig /all" on the Windows Command 
        Prompt. With Network IP and Subnet Mask, Zenmap software was utilized to run a quick "-sn" scan across the entire network for any connected devices. Many
        devices were detected, some of which had device names that provided insight for its purpose. They will later be scanned for open ports in order to
        better explain device type, services, and attack surface. 
  - Asset Correlation
        The "nmap -sn [network IP/subnet]" scan not only returned the device IP addresses, but also device names and websites as well. For instance, some
        phones were titled "iphone.lan([IP])" and laptops were titled "[name]-lt.lan([IP])". Furthermore, the security devices scanned had returned
        their alarm system's website, and IoT devices were titled after the company which manufactured them. Total detected devices included security cameras,
        a doorbell camera, a security control panel, laptops, phones, a printer, a smart TV, an IoT Pool Controller, and an IoT Garage Door Opener.
  - Port Enumeration
        Upon correlating scanned IP addresses with potential susceptible network devices, more aggressive scans were performed in order to find opened ports
        or services running on them. Port and service scans were ran using "-sV" on Zenmap against suspect network devices, where each device's respective
        opened ports alongside the corresponding service would be displayed. This scan was leveraged against the laptop performing the Nmap analysis, security
        devices, IoT devices, and the connected printer. The IoT devices were found susceptible, having ports 23 and 80 opened.
  - Service Detection
        The services running on the opened ports on scanned devices provided important information regarding attack methodology. The IoT devices openly ran
        Telnet (23) and HTTP (80) on the network, leaving them and the rest of the network open to exploitation. Furthermore, the laptop performing Nmap analyses
        had open vmware services running on it over ports 902 and 912. These findings were futher validated through command prompt corroboration using "netstat"
        and "tasklist" commands.
  - IoT Exposure Analysis
        Seeing as though the IoT devices found within the LAN were running unsecure services over open ports across the network, change had to be made in order to harden
        network security. However, the IoT devices required communication with personal devices in order to work properly, so they were still required a network connection.
        Although the Google Nest Router does not supply VLAN capabilities or custom firewall filtering rules, it does allow for creation of a "Guest Network," in which devices 
        can connect to the network and access the Internet, yet remain virtually segmented from main LAN devices. Although the Google Home App allows for a selection of devices
        from the main LAN to be accessed from the Guest Network, such actions would be impractical for network security. In order to harden the network, IoT devices were
        moved to the Guest Network.
  - Endpoint Validation
        something something endpoint validation ??
  - Before/After Segmentation Testing
        IoT devices were segmented to Guest Network in order to mitigate the main LAN's attack surface. An Nmap scan of the Guest Network shows successful IoT connections
        with both devices receiving new IP addresses under the new Guest Network IP class. Successful testing concluded that such devices could still be utilized by personal
        devices through cloud connectivity.

